# Shell scripts must check out with LF even on Windows.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the Windows development box, which gave every script
# in starters/cpp/bench/ryzen/ CRLF endings in the working copy. bash then fails
# on `set -uo pipefail\r` -- and it fails obscurely, because the error names the
# line but not the reason. Pinning eol=lf here fixes it at checkout for anyone
# who clones or re-checks-out; run-harness-windows.sh additionally makes an LF
# copy at run time, which covers a working copy that predates this file.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else executed inside a Linux container.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
